(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Document Object Model \(DOM\) tree architecture, detailing Node hierarchy: Document, Element, Attr, and Text nodes.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Document Object Model \(DOM\) is an object-oriented structural representation of an HTML) Tj
T*
(   document created by the browser during parsing:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   DOM Node Hierarchy:) Tj
T*
(   At the root of the hierarchy is the generic `Node` interface, from which specialized node types) Tj
T*
(   inherit:) Tj
T*
(   1. Document Node: The root owner of the entire document tree \(`window.document`\). Node type) Tj
T*
(   constant = 9 \(`Node.DOCUMENT_NODE`\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Element Nodes: Represent HTML tags \(e.g., `<div>`, `<p>`, `<a>`\). They form the structural) Tj
T*
(   scaffolding of the visual page. Node type = 1 \(`Node.ELEMENT_NODE`\). Elements support) Tj
T*
(   attributes, child nodes, and CSS styling.) Tj
T*
(   3. Text Nodes: Represent the actual text content enclosed within element tags. Node type = 3) Tj
T*
(   \(`Node.TEXT_NODE`\). Text nodes are leaf nodes with no children.) Tj
T*
(   4. Attribute Nodes: Historically represented HTML attributes \(node type = 2\); in modern DOM) Tj
T*
(   specifications, attributes are managed directly on elements via NamedNodeMap) Tj
T*
(   \(`element.attributes`\).) Tj
T*
(   5. Comment Nodes: Represent HTML comments \(`<!-- comment -->`\). Node type = 8) Tj
T*
(   \(`Node.COMMENT_NODE`\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Tree Traversal Properties:) Tj
T*
(   Nodes are navigated using pointer properties: `parentNode`, `childNodes` \(all node types\),) Tj
T*
(   `children` \(elements only\), `firstChild`, `firstElementChild`, `nextSibling`, and) Tj
T*
(   `nextElementSibling`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare DOM selection methods: getElementById, getElementsByTagName, getElementsByClassName, querySelector, and querySelectorAll.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Selecting elements efficiently is fundamental to client-side scripting:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Method Comparison:) Tj
T*
(   1. `getElementById\('id'\)`: Fastest lookup; queries internal hash map for an exact ID match.) Tj
T*
(   Returns a single Element or null.) Tj
T*
(   2. `getElementsByClassName\('class'\)`: Returns a live `HTMLCollection` of elements matching the) Tj
T*
(   class name. Reflects real-time DOM changes automatically.) Tj
T*
(   3. `getElementsByTagName\('tag'\)`: Returns a live `HTMLCollection` of all elements with the) Tj
T*
(   specified tag name \(e.g., 'div'\).) Tj
T*
(   4. `querySelector\('cssSelector'\)`: Evaluates any valid CSS selector string \(including complex) Tj
T*
(   combinators and pseudo-classes\). Returns the FIRST matching Element or null.) Tj
T*
(   5. `querySelectorAll\('cssSelector'\)`: Evaluates CSS selectors and returns a STATIC \(non-live\)) Tj
T*
(   `NodeList` containing all matches snapshot at query time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Performance Criteria: Prefer `getElementById` for simple ID queries due to raw speed. Use) Tj
T*
(   `querySelector` / `querySelectorAll` for rich selector queries \(e.g., `#main >) Tj
T*
(   .card:not\(.hidden\)`\). Note that `NodeList` natively supports `.forEach\(\)`, while) Tj
T*
(   `HTMLCollection` must be converted to an array \(`[...collection]`\) to iterate.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

