(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the complete W3C DOM Event Flow architecture: Capturing Phase, Target Phase, and Bubbling Phase.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   When an event \(such as a mouse click\) occurs on a web element, the browser does not simply) Tj
T*
(   trigger that element's listener immediately; it executes a three-phase event dispatch cycle:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Three Phases:) Tj
T*
(   1. Capturing Phase \(Trickling Down\):) Tj
T*
(   The event originates at the `window` object and travels down through document, html, body, and) Tj
T*
(   successive ancestor elements toward the target element.) Tj
T*
(   - Listeners configured with `{ capture: true }` fire during this phase.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Target Phase:) Tj
T*
(   The event reaches the exact innermost DOM element where the interaction occurred) Tj
T*
(   \(`event.target`\).) Tj
T*
(   - Listeners registered directly on the target element execute in the order they were) Tj
T*
(   registered.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Bubbling Phase \(Bubbling Up\):) Tj
T*
(   The event bubbles upward from the target element through all its ancestor elements back to the) Tj
T*
(   `window`.) Tj
T*
(   - Standard listeners \(default third argument `false`\) fire during this phase.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Stopping Propagation:) Tj
T*
(   - `event.stopPropagation\(\)`: Halts propagation to subsequent ancestors.) Tj
T*
(   - `event.stopImmediatePropagation\(\)`: Halts propagation AND stops any remaining listeners on) Tj
T*
(   the CURRENT element from firing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
