(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Passive Event Listeners \({ passive: true }\) and the performance problems they solve in mobile touch and wheel interactions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Passive event listeners solve critical frame-rate drops during touch scrolling on mobile) Tj
T*
(   devices:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Historical Scrolling Bottleneck:) Tj
T*
(   When a mobile user touches the screen and drags their finger, the browser fires `touchstart`) Tj
T*
(   and `touchmove` events. Because the event listener could potentially call) Tj
T*
(   `event.preventDefault\(\)` to cancel scrolling, the browser MUST pause scrolling completely until) Tj
T*
(   the JavaScript callback finishes executing. If the callback takes 50ms, scrolling stutters) Tj
T*
(   visibly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Passive Solution:) Tj
T*
(   Declaring `{ passive: true }` promises to the browser engine that the listener will NEVER call) Tj
T*
(   `event.preventDefault\(\)`:) Tj
T*
(   ```javascript) Tj
T*
(   window.addEventListener\('touchstart', onTouchStart, { passive: true }\);) Tj
T*
(   window.addEventListener\('wheel', onWheel, { passive: true }\);) Tj
T*
(   ```) Tj
T*
(   Impact: The browser initiates scrolling immediately on the compositor thread without waiting) Tj
T*
(   for the JavaScript main thread, ensuring silky-smooth 60/120 FPS scrolling.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
